Z||000011||MR2KLR||12-07-2001||07:30 AM||||***bump***<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <BR>Black 88 Coupe: 120 MPH spedo, Aux. gauge pod, 195/55VR14 Potenza RE-71 tires, GT exhaust & ground F/X Soon to have 16" White Beretta GTZ W/ 215/45ZR16's<BR><A HREF="http://community-2.webtv.net/TOMTOM81/MYNEWTOY/" TARGET=_blank>Tom's Page</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Raydar||12-07-2001||09:41 AM||||Check for bad grounds. First one to check is the small black wire that comes from the (-) battery post to the metal next to the battery tray. Make sure it's free of corrosion, and tight. Look all over the engine and engine compartment for disconnected wires. Especially black ones. If you find any, you should be able to see where they were supposed to go. <P>What did the previous owner take apart? That's the first place I would look for loose connections. <P>Alternator sounds like it's probably bad, but (and this is a longshot) bad grounds could possibly cause it to not charge the battery. <P>A discharged battery will also make the engine run crappy. As the battery voltage drops, the ECM will attempt to compensate, and will get confused due to the sensor readings it's getting. The DIS also needs a bunch of current to work properly. <P>I've seen evidence of the same water leak on my 88 coupe. The water pump is high up on the right front corner of the engine. The pump could be leaking, or the hose clamp could be loose, at the pump outlet. I believe there are also some heater hose connections near that corner of the cradle. Check for loose clamps, etc.<P><BR>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 12-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Kasdan||12-07-2001||01:30 PM||||Could number 1 be a bad fuel pump sending unit?  thats what mine does, and my mechanic said that was the problem..or if he FOS?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Indiana_resto_guy||12-07-2001||01:55 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by MR2KLR:<BR><B>I was just out leting the car get hot I noticed that it starts to run crappy when the batt gets low. BUT the battery allways had enough juice to start the car. I have had a bad alt B4 and the car would stall out when the battery would die. How much does a new alt. cost for a 88? <P>I grounded the dark green wire like my haynes manual sad to test the temp switch nothing happend.<P>while the car was running I unpluged the coolant switch (in the water neck), The fan came on along with the check engine light the car also ran a bit better (less gurgleing at idle)<P>as for the water pump will it be OK for a while as long at I keep water in it. or do I NEED on right now?<P>Thanks<BR>Tom</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Alternator will run from $90.00 and up.<P>Check the function for all temperature functions. sounds like one is shorted out and affecting the rest of the circuit.<P>Replace the water pump ASAP! If you don't, you may find yourself shopping for a head! (experience speaking!)<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||JohnF||12-07-2001||06:32 PM||||Somebody help me here?  I have an 2.5 '88 and the fan only comes on when I select AC.  That's what was originally described and that's what my car has done since I bought it new 13 years ago.<P>Am I missing something that he is being told?  Item #4 on his list looks like normal operation.<P>Items 1 thru 3 could certainly be related to low voltage at the ECM and should be eliminated as a cause before he tries anything else but, number 4??????<P>I live in Texas and have never had the fan come on because of temperature.  He lives in MD, it'll NEVER get hot enough there if the car is properly maintained.<P>Certainly my 2 cents worth!  <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1578096&a=12001112&p=56509969&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||10
